+#### Sensitivity of KCOR to the Gompertz slope {#gompertz-slope-sensitivity}
+
+Hold the observed cumulative hazards $H_d(t)$ and the fitting weeks fixed. Let $\hat\theta_d(\gamma)$ be the frailty variance from jointly refitting the baseline rate and variance at weekly slope $\gamma$. For proportional inversion (§2.6), write $F(H,\theta)=(e^{\theta H}-1)/\theta$. Its fractional response to a change in variance, for $H>0$, is
+
+$$
+D(H,\theta)=\frac{\partial\log F(H,\theta)}{\partial\theta}
+=\frac{H}{1-e^{-\theta H}}-\frac{1}{\theta},
+\qquad D(H,0)=H/2.
+$$
+
+Write $D_d(t)=D(H_d(t),\hat\theta_d)$ and $\hat\theta'_d=d\hat\theta_d/d\gamma$. Wherever the refit varies smoothly with $\gamma$, differentiating the log ratio gives
+
+$$
+\frac{d\log\mathrm{KCOR}(t)}{d\gamma}
+=D_A(t)\,\hat\theta'_A-D_B(t)\,\hat\theta'_B.
+$$
+
+For KCOR anchored at $t_0$, replace each $D_d(t)$ by $D_d(t)-D_d(t_0)$. First-order cancellation occurs when the two cohort contributions are equal.
+
+For example, suppose both cohorts have constant observed hazard $c=0.001$ per week for 20 quiet weeks. Then $k=c$ and $\hat\theta=\gamma/c$ fit both cohorts exactly (§2.4.4). If A's hazard doubles for the next 20 weeks while B's stays constant, their week-40 cumulative hazards are $60c$ and $40c$. Anchoring at week 20 gives $(e^{60\gamma}-1)/(e^{40\gamma}-1)$. Changing the annual slope from 0.085 to 0.065 or 0.105 moves this ratio from 1.5251 to 1.5191 or 1.5311, respectively. These values describe the specified hazard histories.
+
 ### S5.4 Quiet-window perturbation scan (empirical Czech data)
 
 To assess whether diagnostically valid quiet windows are rare or fragile in applied registry data, we performed a systematic scan of quiet-window placements in the Czech Republic 2021–2024 cohort (enrollment ISO week 2021-24). We re-estimated the revised frailty parameters $(\hat{k}_d,\hat{\theta}_{0,d})$ across a sequence of overlapping quiet windows of fixed duration, holding all other settings fixed.
